  "id": "Compound::DB01073 - Disease::DOID:2531",
  "verbalization": "Fludarabine is implicated in the pathogenesis of Hematologic Cancer through a series of interconnected mechanisms. The evidence suggests that Fludarabine, as a treatment for Lymphatic System Cancer, leads to the treatment of Hematologic Cancer via Cytarabine or Mechlorethamine, both of which are classified as cytotoxic chemotherapeutic agents. This indicates a potential pathway where Fludarabine, through its treatment of Lymphatic System Cancer, indirectly contributes to the treatment of Hematologic Cancer by targeting the underlying disease processes. The shared lowest common ancestor between Fludarabine and these agents, as an antimetabolite, suggests a common biochemical pathway that may underlie their therapeutic effects. While the direct causal link between Fludarabine and Hematologic Cancer is not explicitly stated, the cumulative evidence points to a role in the treatment of this condition through the activation of cytotoxic agents. The integration of these pathways highlights the importance of Fludarabine in the context of hematologic malignancies, particularly through its involvement in the treatment of lymphatic cancers that ultimately lead to hematologic disease.",
